WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.040
Speaker 1: Everyone, welcome to the David
Parkman Show.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.230
I'm producer Pat Forde, filling in for David
today as he is traveling in California.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.679
We're going to have guest host for the remainder
of the week.

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:14.450
So some pretty big news on the Donald Trump
indictment front happened over the weekend.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.869
Donald Trump was interviewed by Brett Becker
on Fox News and he couldn't get through the

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:25.820
20 minute or so exchange without incriminating
himself multiple times, admitting to doing

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:29.240
the very things that he's been accused of
in this federal indictment.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.730
Donald Trump has been doing media interviews.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.989
He's been doing these rallies where he talks
about the criminal charges and basically admits

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:45.010
to the fact that he had these classified documents
in his possession and obstructed justice thereafter.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:50.190
This is, of course, a terrible idea for any
criminal defendant, any counsel.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.399
Defense lawyers would advise you not to speak
about the case.

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:59.070
But because Donald Trump is running for president
and he needs to at all times appear like he's

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.100
a fighter, he believes he's smarter than everyone
else.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.189
He is deciding to just do it anyway, to talk
about the case.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:11.659
And what he's been doing really is giving
himself more and more rope to hang himself

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:12.659
with.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:16.110
And that's because, generally speaking, the
prosecution can use such statements that you

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:23.270
make in the media to establish your guilt
or to try to undermine your defense arguments.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.750
And legally, it can get more complicated,
of course, because you get into a conversation

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.729
about what is and what isn't admissible in
court, what does and what doesn't qualify

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:31.760
as hearsay.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.500
But there's a strong case that these media
interviews, like the one we're about to look

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.990
at by Trump, will end up being used as exhibits
in his upcoming trial.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.140
So the bottom line is, this is why you stay
silent.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.040
I think it was Ron Phillip Koski on Twitter
who said that if the 500 best lawyers in the

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.400
entire world told Donald Trump to stay silent
on this matter, he would disregard them thinking

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.049
that he was smarter than all of them.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:02:01.000
And then MAGA would use that as justification
to vote for him because they would think that

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.130
that's some sort of qualification for being
president, even though that would actually

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.720
make him the worst possible choice to be president.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:13.590
Zero in on the most damaging things that Donald
Trump said during this interview, Trump admits

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:19.260
to taking the documents down to Mar a Lago
with him and that he had these boxes of documents

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:25.330
in his possession that they were interspersed
with his personal possessions.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:31.019
And this is all going to the charge of willful
retention of national defense information.

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:32.970
He's admitting to the whole thing here.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:33.970
Let's take a look at the clip.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:40.900
